<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tell Tayinat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kunulua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tell Tayinat is an archaeological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuq-valley">Amuq plain</a> of Hatay, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, identified with Kunulua, capital of the Neo-Hittite kingdom of Patina (Unqi). Excavations have uncovered palaces, temples, Luwian inscriptions and a copy of the succession treaty of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a>, from the period after Tiglath-Pileser III made it an Assyrian provincial centre in 738 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tell-tayinat.jpg" alt="Tell Tayinat" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tayinat Archaeological Project - credit Murat Akar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tayinat_Flyover_(Photo_credit_Murat_Akar).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
